use Illuminate\Support\Facades\DB;
use Illuminate\Support\Str;
class DatabaseSeeder extends Seeder
{
    public function run(): void
    {
        if (Setting::where('name', 'installed')->exists()) {
            return;
        }
        DB::transaction(function () {

            Setting::put('demo','1');
            Setting::put('brief_title','A little perspective in a market that never sleeps.');
            Setting::put('brief_body','Start with the bigger picture. Look at liquidity, understand the catalyst, and separate a short-term price move from a long-term change. This preview edition shows how your daily briefing will look.');
            Setting::put('brief_points',"Read beyond the headline: check the original announcement.\nWatch the calendar: upgrades and unlocks deserve context.\nThink in scenarios: a thesis should have a clear invalidation point.");
            Setting::put('brief_updated','2026-10-14 08:30:00');
            Setting::put('media_email','');
            Setting::put('media_audience','Crypto-curious readers, independent researchers, and people who want a clearer view of digital assets.');
            $content=[
            ['Beyond the price: how to read a crypto market','Analysis','Bitcoin','A better market view starts with liquidity, context, and the questions a price chart cannot answer.',"A green candle tells you that the last traded price rose. It does not tell you who bought, how deep the market was, or whether the move can last. A useful market review begins by putting the price in context.\n\n## Start with the timeframe\nAn hourly move and a six-month trend answer different questions. Decide which timeframe your research concerns before comparing charts. Record the date and source of every observation so that later readers can judge its relevance.\n\n## Look at participation\nVolume describes trading activity, but reported volume alone does not establish liquidity. Spreads, order-book depth, and the venues contributing to a move can matter. Market capitalisation is price multiplied by circulating supply; it is not the amount of cash invested in an asset.\n\n## Separate the catalyst from the narrative\nAn upgrade, governance decision, or supply change can be documented. Claims about how the market will react remain interpretations. Read primary announcements and distinguish what has happened from what is expected.\n\n## Build more than one scenario\nDescribe what would support your view and what would challenge it. A good research note can be revised when evidence changes. This guide is an educational framework, not a current market forecast.",'https://www.coingecko.com/en/methodology'],
            ['Bitcoin, explained without the noise','Learn','Bitcoin','The network, the asset, and the ideas behind the original cryptocurrency.',"Bitcoin is a peer-to-peer system for transferring value without a central transaction processor. Its public ledger lets participants verify transactions and the rules governing issuance.\n\n## The network and the asset\nBitcoin refers to both a network and its native asset, BTC. A wallet manages keys that can authorise transactions; it does not hold coins like files on a device. Losing access to those keys can mean losing access to funds.\n\n## Why supply matters\nBitcoin’s issuance schedule is defined by its consensus rules. Miner rewards decline at scheduled block intervals. This is different from a guarantee that demand or the market price will rise.\n\n## What to read next\nLearn how addresses, transaction fees, confirmations, and self-custody work before moving funds. Begin with official documentation and practise with small amounts where appropriate.",'https://bitcoin.org/en/how-it-works'],
            ['Ethereum is more than a cryptocurrency','Learn','Ethereum','A practical introduction to smart contracts, applications, and the role of ETH.',"Ethereum is a programmable blockchain. Its smart contracts can support applications that execute according to published code, although users still need to understand the risks of the application and its dependencies.\n\n## What ETH does\nETH is Ethereum’s native asset. It is used for transaction fees and to participate in the network’s proof-of-stake security. Application tokens are separate assets with their own design and risks.\n\n## Understanding layers\nLayer 2 systems aim to handle activity outside the main execution environment while using Ethereum in different ways for settlement or security. Their assumptions and withdrawal processes vary.\n\n## Before connecting a wallet\nRead the application’s documentation, verify the domain, and understand the permissions you are approving. A smart contract audit is useful evidence but does not eliminate risk.",'https://ethereum.org/en/what-is-ethereum/'],
            ['Token unlocks: the calendar event worth understanding','Analysis','Altcoins','An unlock changes availability. Understanding who receives tokens adds the missing context.',"A token unlock makes previously restricted tokens available under a project’s allocation schedule. It is a supply event, not an automatic instruction to buy or sell.\n\n## Ask who receives the tokens\nAllocations may go to contributors, investors, a treasury, or community programmes. Their restrictions and likely uses can differ. Check original tokenomics documents rather than relying solely on a headline amount.\n\n## Compare the right quantities\nLook at the unlock relative to circulating supply and available liquidity. A dollar estimate based on the latest price can change quickly and should carry a timestamp.\n\n## Recheck the schedule\nGovernance decisions and project announcements can alter expectations. Our calendar supports source links and verification dates so that readers can inspect the basis for an entry.",'https://ethereum.org/en/whitepaper/'],
            ['How to verify a crypto announcement','News','Altcoins','A practical checklist for separating a primary announcement from an unverified claim.',"A screenshot can circulate faster than its source. Before treating a crypto announcement as established fact, work backwards to the original statement.\n\n## Find the original source\nCheck the project’s official site and documentation. Verify that a social account is linked from an established official domain. A lookalike name or a paid verification badge is not sufficient evidence.\n\n## Read the details\nA proposal is different from an approved decision. A test network release is different from a production deployment. Describe the exact stage and include its date.\n\n## Keep a correction trail\nIf the source changes or an earlier report is wrong, update the article and explain the correction. This is a sample editorial guide, not a breaking-news report.",'https://ethereum.org/en/security/'],
            ['Stablecoins: what does “stable” actually mean?','Analysis','Altcoins','The peg is only the beginning. Reserves, redemption, and design all deserve attention.',"Stablecoins aim to track a reference value, often a fiat currency. Different designs rely on different collateral, counterparties, and mechanisms.\n\n## Look behind the peg\nFor reserve-backed assets, examine what supports the token and who can redeem it. For other designs, understand how collateral and incentives behave when markets become stressed.\n\n## Read the terms\nAttestations, redemption rules, custody arrangements, and jurisdiction are all relevant. A token’s market price can diverge from its intended reference value.\n\n## Understand the chain\nThe same asset name may appear on multiple networks or in bridged forms. Verify the contract and issuer’s supported network list before interacting.",'https://ethereum.org/en/stablecoins/'],
            ['Your first wallet: a security-first guide','Learn','Bitcoin','Understand recovery phrases, approvals, and the habits that protect access.',"A crypto wallet is a tool for managing keys and signing transactions. The most important setup step is understanding how recovery works before funds are involved.\n\n## Protect the recovery phrase\nFollow the wallet provider’s official instructions. Do not share a recovery phrase in chat, enter it into unsolicited forms, or treat a support message as trustworthy simply because it uses familiar branding.\n\n## Check before signing\nVerify the website, network, destination, and requested permissions. A signature may authorise something beyond a simple transfer.\n\n## Plan for recovery\nConsider how you would recover access if a device were lost. Keep backups protected and avoid storing secrets in places exposed to routine phishing or account compromise.",'https://bitcoin.org/en/secure-your-wallet','https://ethereum.org/en/security/'],
            ['Layer 1 vs Layer 2: a clear comparison','Learn','Altcoins','Where transactions happen, where they settle, and why the distinction matters.',"A Layer 1 is a base blockchain with its own consensus system. A Layer 2 is built to extend a base network, but the term covers designs with meaningful differences.\n\n## Execution and settlement\nAsk where transactions execute, where data is available, and how the result is validated or challenged. These details explain more than a simple transaction-per-second claim.\n\n## Bridges add assumptions\nMoving assets between networks can involve contracts, delays, and additional security assumptions. Review the supported withdrawal path and official documentation.\n\n## Compare trade-offs\nFees, decentralisation, developer tooling, security, and user experience all matter. No single metric provides a complete comparison.",'https://ethereum.org/en/layer-2/'],
            ['Market cap is not the same as money invested','Analysis','Bitcoin','A common metric, a common misunderstanding, and a more useful way to read it.',"Market capitalisation is generally calculated by multiplying a token’s price by its circulating supply. A small trade can change the reference price without an equivalent amount of new cash entering the market.\n\n## Circulating vs fully diluted\nFully diluted valuation uses a broader supply measure. Understand the provider’s definitions, particularly when token supply can change or allocation schedules are complex.\n\n## Liquidity still matters\nA high valuation does not guarantee that a large order can be executed near the quoted price. Slippage and market depth are separate questions.\n\n## Use metrics together\nRead market cap alongside supply disclosures, turnover, venue coverage, and project fundamentals. Record the source and timestamp of any numerical comparison.",'https://www.coingecko.com/en/methodology'],
            ['The research notebook: a calmer way to follow crypto','News','Bitcoin','A simple routine for keeping evidence, questions, and interpretations separate.',"An organised research notebook can make a noisy market easier to follow. Write down what you know, what you think, and what you still need to verify.\n\n## Keep an evidence column\nSave original links with dates. A quotation without its context can easily mislead. Note whether a statement is a completed action, a proposal, or an expectation.\n\n## Track your questions\nQuestions about liquidity, token ownership, technical changes, and governance often reveal the next useful research step.\n\n## Review rather than react\nReturn to earlier notes when new evidence appears. Updating a view is part of research. This sample edition demonstrates the type of evergreen coverage Compyvila can publish.",'https://bitcoin.org/en/resources'],
            ];
            foreach($content as $i=>$a) {
                [$title,$category,$coin,$excerpt,$body]=$a;
                Article::firstOrCreate(['slug'=>Str::slug($title)], ['title'=>$title,'excerpt'=>$excerpt,'body'=>$body,'category'=>$category,'coin'=>$coin,'image'=>'','sources'=>implode("\n",array_slice($a,5)),'status'=>'published','featured'=>$i===0,'published_at'=>'2026-10-'.str_pad((string)(14-min($i,9)),2,'0',STR_PAD_LEFT).' 08:00:00','updated_at'=>'2026-10-14 08:00:00']);
            }
            foreach([['BTC','Bitcoin',64000,1.84],['ETH','Ethereum',2600,0.72],['SOL','Solana',150,-1.23],['BNB','BNB',570,0.46]] as $c) Snapshot::firstOrCreate(['symbol'=>$c[0]], ['name'=>$c[1],'price'=>$c[2],'change_pct'=>$c[3],'captured_at'=>'2026-10-14 08:30:00','source'=>'']);
            foreach([
            ['Weekly research review','Review primary announcements, update your research notes, and revisit the evidence behind your market view.','2026-10-16 18:00:00','Editorial',''],
            ['Bitcoin reading session','An editorial learning session using the original Bitcoin documentation. Sample calendar entry, not an external event.','2026-10-18 16:00:00','Learn','https://bitcoin.org/en/resources'],
            ['Ethereum ecosystem notebook','Our scheduled review of Ethereum documentation and ecosystem topics. Sample editorial calendar entry.','2026-10-21 10:00:00','Editorial','https://ethereum.org/en/'],
            ] as $event) Event::create(array_combine(['title','description','event_at','category','source','verified_at'], [...$event,'2026-10-14 08:30:00']));
            Setting::put('installed', '1');
        });
    }
